# Binary assets, marked so nothing can rewrite them on the way in or out.
#
# Git's heuristic usually gets this right on its own, and "usually" is the
# problem: a checkout with `core.autocrlf` set — the default on Windows, and
# easy to inherit into a Linux config — rewrites line endings in anything Git
# decides is text. A PNG's own signature contains a CR and an LF, and a texture
# that is one byte different is a texture that does not decode. The symptom is
# a world drawn entirely in the missing-texture chequer, with nothing in any log
# to say why: the decode failure reaches a warning the console never shows.
#
# Marking them explicitly costs nothing and removes the heuristic from the
# question.
*.png   binary
*.wav   binary
*.ogg   binary
*.glb   binary
*.gltf  binary
*.bin   binary
*.sqlite binary

# Text that must stay LF whatever the platform, because its bytes are hashed or
# parsed by the engine rather than only read by a person.
*.lua   text eol=lf
*.rs    text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.wgsl  text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
